{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VapA8oPMhLax1R3kshNXfc","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# docs/stack.ini — the kit meta-repo's own product toolchain, declared once.\n2\t#\n3\t# The \"product\" this repo builds is the kit under project-trajectory/; its tests\n4\t# live in tests/ (they bootstrap a real scaffold and exercise every script). This\n5\t# is the meta-repo's SELF-adoption of the process it ships — IMPROVEMENT_PLAN.md\n6\t# Thread 47. scripts under project-trajectory/scripts/check.py read this file.\n7\t\n8\t[paths]\n9\tsrc = project-trajectory/scripts\n10\ttests = tests\n11\t\n12\t[product]\n13\tformat = {py} -m ruff format --check {src} {tests}\n14\tlint = {py} -m ruff check {src} {tests}\n15\t# `-n auto` runs the suite across all cores via pytest-xdist (WI-075). Every test\n16\t# is isolated in tmp_path and the meta-tree readers are read-only, so the suite is\n17\t# xdist-safe; subprocess coverage (tests/conftest.augment_env) holds per-worker\n18\t# (measured combined total 90.8%, unchanged from serial). Measured 2026-07-22 on\n19\t# a 24-core box: ~250 s parallel for the full 1374-test suite (~200 s for the\n20\t# smoke tier); the coverage step runs several times longer. These are snapshots,\n21\t# not contracts — re-measure locally before budgeting against them (the prior\n22\t# ~65 s figure predated the v4 dispatch/integrate suite growth and misled\n23\t# session budgeting for weeks; repo-review 2026-07-21 M-24).\n24\t# The `smoke` tier stays as declared; the coverage args below still apply.\n25\ttest = {py} -m pytest -q -n auto\n26\t\n27\t[tiers]\n28\tsmoke = -m smoke\n29\tfull = -m \"not release\"\n30\trelease =\n31\tall =\n32\t\n33\t[coverage]\n34\t# Subprocess coverage IS wired (Thread 47 Phase 6). This suite exercises the kit\n35\t# scripts as SUBPROCESSES (bootstrap a temp scaffold, run the real command), which\n36\t# `--cov` would otherwise miss, so tests/conftest.run_py starts coverage in each\n37\t# child (COVERAGE_PROCESS_START + tests/_cov/augment_env) and .coveragerc's\n38\t# [paths] folds each temp-scaffold */scripts/ copy back onto the source tree, while\n39\t# its [report] omit drops the temp-dir test fixtures so the number is the PRODUCT's.\n40\t# Product coverage measures ~91% on the PINNED toolchain (requirements-dev.txt,\n41\t# WI-104: pytest-cov ~=7, uniform now the kit floor is 3.11 — WI-262). The floor is 85 — a real\n42\t# REGRESSION SENSOR, not a formality: WI-105 (the 2026-07-12b review's M9). The pin\n43\t# fixed the flaky combine race that had degraded the measurement to ~82% (children\n44\t# silently lost from the combine on the old-local pytest-cov 4.1.0), but an 80 floor\n45\t# would have passed that ~82% silently — \"1.83 points of plumbing luck\". At 85 a\n46\t# renewed silent subprocess-coverage loss (~91% -> ~82%) turns the gate RED instead,\n47\t# with ~6 points of headroom for legitimate variation on both pinned Pythons. NB: an\n48\t# UNPINNED toolchain still reads the degraded ~82% and now fails here — that red is\n49\t# the intended nudge to `sh scripts/dev-setup.sh --install` onto the pinned tools.\n50\tthreshold = 85\n51\targs = --cov={src} --cov-report=term-missing --cov-fail-under={coverage}\n52\t\n53\t[step:dupes]\n54\t# Duplicate-code gate (WI-078; check.py extra_steps). Fails on any copy-paste\n55\t# block of >= --min-tokens significant tokens between a file-PAIR not recorded\n56\t# in the census docs/dupes-allow (the default --allowlist path). The census\n57\t# grandfathers the current F5 small-helper duplication + the known intra-file\n58\t# debris; new duplication between an unlisted pair turns the G3 gate RED. Runs\n59\t# at G3 with the other product-layer quality gates (format/lint/tests).\n60\tcommand = {py} project-trajectory/scripts/check_dupes.py --src {src}\n61\tgates = G3\n62\tlayer = product\n63\t\n64\t[arch-map]\n65\t# Symbol map over project-trajectory/scripts (Phase 6 authors docs/architecture.md).\n66\tmode = symbols\n67\t\n68\t[generated]\n69\t# The parallel-dispatch integrator's auto-resolution allowlist (WI-235): a\n70\t# composition conflict CONFINED to one of these generated artifacts is resolved\n71\t# by REGENERATING from the merged sources instead of parking. agent_dispatch.py\n72\t# reads THIS section (the integrate worktree's own copy); an ABSENT section falls\n73\t# back to the byte-identical built-in defaults, a MALFORMED row fails closed to\n74\t# park. Each row: <path> = <kind> [| <BEGIN> | <END>]; kinds are\n75\t# trajectory | okf | status | archmap; a \"/\"-terminated path is a prefix. The\n76\t# skills index is deliberately absent (its per-agent copies are hand-authored\n77\t# source that must park, not regenerate — WI-231).\n78\tPROJECT_STATE.html = trajectory\n79\tdocs/okf/ = okf\n80\tdocs/architecture.md = archmap | <!-- BEGIN GENERATED MODULE MAP --> | <!-- END GENERATED MODULE MAP -->\n81\tdocs/status.md = status | <!-- BEGIN GENERATED STATUS --> | <!-- END GENERATED STATUS -->\n82\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7389bb9f-9f9a-499d-87c4-4c422632b100","uuid":"b91d6bb7-892d-476e-8072-877b4835809a","timestamp":"2026-07-23T07:42:00.044Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Projects\\ai-template-trains\\p0-g3-WI-281-9ae9\\docs\\stack.ini","content":"# docs/stack.ini — the kit meta-repo's own product toolchain, declared once.\n#\n# The \"product\" this repo builds is the kit under project-trajectory/; its tests\n# live in tests/ (they bootstrap a real scaffold and exercise every script). This\n# is the meta-repo's SELF-adoption of the process it ships — IMPROVEMENT_PLAN.md\n# Thread 47. scripts under project-trajectory/scripts/check.py read this file.\n\n[paths]\nsrc = project-trajectory/scripts\ntests = tests\n\n[product]\nformat = {py} -m ruff format --check {src} {tests}\nlint = {py} -m ruff check {src} {tests}\n# `-n auto` runs the suite across all cores via pytest-xdist (WI-075). Every test\n# is isolated in tmp_path and the meta-tree readers are read-only, so the suite is\n# xdist-safe; subprocess coverage (tests/conftest.augment_env) holds per-worker\n# (measured combined total 90.8%, unchanged from serial). Measured 2026-07-22 on\n# a 24-core box: ~250 s parallel for the full 1374-test suite (~200 s for the\n# smoke tier); the coverage step runs several times longer. These are snapshots,\n# not contracts — re-measure locally before budgeting against them (the prior\n# ~65 s figure predated the v4 dispatch/integrate suite growth and misled\n# session budgeting for weeks; repo-review 2026-07-21 M-24).\n# The `smoke` tier stays as declared; the coverage args below still apply.\ntest = {py} -m pytest -q -n auto\n\n[tiers]\nsmoke = -m smoke\nfull = -m \"not release\"\nrelease =\nall =\n\n[coverage]\n# Subprocess coverage IS wired (Thread 47 Phase 6). This suite exercises the kit\n# scripts as SUBPROCESSES (bootstrap a temp scaffold, run the real command), which\n# `--cov` would otherwise miss, so tests/conftest.run_py starts coverage in each\n# child (COVERAGE_PROCESS_START + tests/_cov/augment_env) and .coveragerc's\n# [paths] folds each temp-scaffold */scripts/ copy back onto the source tree, while\n# its [report] omit drops the temp-dir test fixtures so the number is the PRODUCT's.\n# Product coverage measures ~91% on the PINNED toolchain (requirements-dev.txt,\n# WI-104: pytest-cov ~=7, uniform now the kit floor is 3.11 — WI-262). The floor is 85 — a real\n# REGRESSION SENSOR, not a formality: WI-105 (the 2026-07-12b review's M9). The pin\n# fixed the flaky combine race that had degraded the measurement to ~82% (children\n# silently lost from the combine on the old-local pytest-cov 4.1.0), but an 80 floor\n# would have passed that ~82% silently — \"1.83 points of plumbing luck\". At 85 a\n# renewed silent subprocess-coverage loss (~91% -> ~82%) turns the gate RED instead,\n# with ~6 points of headroom for legitimate variation on both pinned Pythons. NB: an\n# UNPINNED toolchain still reads the degraded ~82% and now fails here — that red is\n# the intended nudge to `sh scripts/dev-setup.sh --install` onto the pinned tools.\nthreshold = 85\nargs = --cov={src} --cov-report=term-missing --cov-fail-under={coverage}\n\n[step:dupes]\n# Duplicate-code gate (WI-078; check.py extra_steps). Fails on any copy-paste\n# block of >= --min-tokens significant tokens between a file-PAIR not recorded\n# in the census docs/dupes-allow (the default --allowlist path). The census\n# grandfathers the current F5 small-helper duplication + the known intra-file\n# debris; new duplication between an unlisted pair turns the G3 gate RED. Runs\n# at G3 with the other product-layer quality gates (format/lint/tests).\ncommand = {py} project-trajectory/scripts/check_dupes.py --src {src}\ngates = G3\nlayer = product\n\n[arch-map]\n# Symbol map over project-trajectory/scripts (Phase 6 authors docs/architecture.md).\nmode = symbols\n\n[generated]\n# The parallel-dispatch integrator's auto-resolution allowlist (WI-235): a\n# composition conflict CONFINED to one of these generated artifacts is resolved\n# by REGENERATING from the merged sources instead of parking. agent_dispatch.py\n# reads THIS section (the integrate worktree's own copy); an ABSENT section falls\n# back to the byte-identical built-in defaults, a MALFORMED row fails closed to\n# park. Each row: <path> = <kind> [| <BEGIN> | <END>]; kinds are\n# trajectory | okf | status | archmap; a \"/\"-terminated path is a prefix. The\n# skills index is deliberately absent (its per-agent copies are hand-authored\n# source that must park, not regenerate — WI-231).\nPROJECT_STATE.html = trajectory\ndocs/okf/ = okf\ndocs/a
[... byte cap hit — full stream in out/run-logs/ ...]
irect"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":456,"cache_read_input_tokens":209086,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":456},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7389bb9f-9f9a-499d-87c4-4c422632b100","uuid":"18729770-6096-4da1-ac7c-ad909916a0c7","request_id":"req_011CdJeq6tr389JQJou5bBKC"}
